{
  "duration": 443.83200000001233,
  "chapters": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "end": 85.15200000000233
    },
    {
      "start": 85.15200000000233,
      "end": 171.5520000000047
    },
    {
      "start": 171.5520000000047,
      "end": 260.7600000000072
    },
    {
      "start": 260.7600000000072,
      "end": 348.2160000000096
    },
    {
      "start": 348.2160000000096,
      "end": 443.83200000001233
    }
  ],
  "cues": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "end": 2.2799999713897705,
      "text": "Hey, welcome back.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 3.240000009536743,
      "end": 6.539999961853027,
      "text": "Last time, a magnetic field bent the path of a moving charge.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 7.619999885559082,
      "end": 12,
      "text": "Today, many moving charges are inside a conducting wire, and their magnetic",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 12,
      "end": 13.680000305175781,
      "text": "forces are transferred to the conductor.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 14.479999542236328,
      "end": 16,
      "text": "The wire can bend or move.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 16.920000076293945,
      "end": 21.299999237060547,
      "text": "For a tiny current element, the force is I times d l cross the external",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 21.299999237060547,
      "end": 22.100000381469727,
      "text": "magnetic field.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 22.81999969482422,
      "end": 25.600000381469727,
      "text": "The vector d l points along conventional current.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 26.34000015258789,
      "end": 30.56358934060121,
      "text": "In a metal, electrons drift the opposite way, but we do not reverse the",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 30.56358934060121,
      "end": 30.979999542236328,
      "text": "current",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 30.979999542236328,
      "end": 32.439998626708984,
      "text": "arrow again in this formula.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 33.86000061035156,
      "end": 37.29999923706055,
      "text": "The negative charge and opposite drift direction are already accounted for.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 38.02000045776367,
      "end": 42.2599983215332,
      "text": "For a straight segment in a uniform field, add all the elements to get I times",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 42.2599983215332,
      "end": 43.560001373291016,
      "text": "the length vector cross B.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 44.36000061035156,
      "end": 48.71882292803596,
      "text": "Its magnitude is I L B sine theta, where theta is between current direction",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 48.71882292803596,
      "end": 49.29999923706055,
      "text": "and field.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 50.2400016784668,
      "end": 52.2599983215332,
      "text": "Picture current to the right with B into the page.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 52.900001525878906,
      "end": 55.540000915527344,
      "text": "Right cross into the page gives upward force on the wire.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 56.65999984741211,
      "end": 58.68000030517578,
      "text": "Reverse only the current and force becomes downward.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 59.79999923706055,
      "end": 62.2400016784668,
      "text": "Reverse only the field and it also becomes downward.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 63.20000076293945,
      "end": 65.31999969482422,
      "text": "Reverse both and the force returns upward.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 66.18000030517578,
      "end": 69.22000122070312,
      "text": "A wire parallel to B has zero magnetic force in this model.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 70.26000213623047,
      "end": 73.95999908447266,
      "text": "Always identify the particular wire and the field produced by other sources.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 74.9800033569336,
      "end": 78.91999816894531,
      "text": "We are not inserting the wire's own field into a formula for its net sideways",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 78.91999816894531,
      "end": 79.30000305175781,
      "text": "force.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 80.36000061035156,
      "end": 84.5999984741211,
      "text": "Draw current, field and force as three separate directions before reaching for",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 84.5999984741211,
      "end": 84.83999633789062,
      "text": "numbers.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 85.15200000000233,
      "end": 88.47199993324513,
      "text": "Let's calculate a force before building a more complicated circuit.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 89.6120000381493,
      "end": 93.60687220470955,
      "text": "A straight forty centimeter segment carries three amperes in a uniform field",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 93.60687220470955,
      "end": 93.71200041961903,
      "text": "of",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 93.71200041961903,
      "end": 95.13199954223866,
      "text": "zero point two zero tesla.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 96.25200038147206,
      "end": 97.41200022888417,
      "text": "Current and field are perpendicular.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 98.65200000000233,
      "end": 101.0519996185326,
      "text": "Convert forty centimeters to zero point four zero meters.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 102.09200053405995,
      "end": 104.83200030517811,
      "text": "Multiply three by zero point four by zero point two.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 105.83200030517811,
      "end": 107.77200083923573,
      "text": "The force is zero point two four newtons.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 108.91200022888417,
      "end": 111.75200038147206,
      "text": "Now let the angle between current and field be thirty degrees.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 112.5519996185326,
      "end": 116.63199954223866,
      "text": "Sine thirty is one half, so the force becomes zero point one two newtons.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 117.89200167846913,
      "end": 122.81199984741444,
      "text": "Be careful if a diagram labels the complementary angle: first find the actual",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 122.81199984741444,
      "end": 123.99200015259022,
      "text": "angle between current and B.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 124.85200076294178,
      "end": 127.57199816894764,
      "text": "Only the length inside the field belongs in the simple expression.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 129.13199954223865,
      "end": 132.53200106811755,
      "text": "Suppose a horizontal wire has mass twelve grams and length thirty centimeters",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 132.672000457766,
      "end": 135.79199938965075,
      "text": "in a perpendicular field of zero point four zero tesla.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 137.0919986267113,
      "end": 140.672000457766,
      "text": "Assume negligible forces from connecting leads and use gravitational",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 140.672000457766,
      "end": 142.97199969482654,
      "text": "acceleration ten meters per second squared.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 144.4319987792992,
      "end": 145.97199969482654,
      "text": "Weight is zero point one two newtons.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 147.172000457766,
      "end": 151.97199969482654,
      "text": "For magnetic force to support the wire, set I L B equal to m g.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 151.97199969482654,
      "end": 154.07199816894763,
      "text": "The required current magnitude is one ampere.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 154.49199633789294,
      "end": 155.2519984741234,
      "text": "Is that the whole answer?",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 156.4120021362328,
      "end": 157.47199969482654,
      "text": "Pause and check the arrows.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 158.67199664306872,
      "end": 162.49199633789294,
      "text": "With field into the page, current must be rightward to produce upward support.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 163.27200274658435,
      "end": 166.87200122070544,
      "text": "A leftward current of the same magnitude would add a downward magnetic force",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 166.87200122070544,
      "end": 167.2519984741234,
      "text": "instead.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 167.2519984741234,
      "end": 170.619693622298,
      "text": "A correct number paired with the wrong direction does not describe",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 170.619693622298,
      "end": 171.232001831057,
      "text": "equilibrium.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 171.5520000000047,
      "end": 174.41199989510054,
      "text": "Does a bent wire require a new force law?",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 175.01200003815168,
      "end": 175.29200000954145,
      "text": "No.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 176.1719998855638,
      "end": 178.57199998093122,
      "text": "Add I d l cross B along its path.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 179.27199979019636,
      "end": 182.74276879208375,
      "text": "If current is steady and the external field is uniform everywhere on the",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 182.74276879208375,
      "end": 183.03199954224104,
      "text": "chosen",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 183.03199954224104,
      "end": 186.09199996185774,
      "text": "wire, I and B can be taken outside the integral.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 186.69200034332746,
      "end": 190.41200061035627,
      "text": "The sum of the tiny displacement vectors is the vector from starting endpoint",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 190.41200061035627,
      "end": 193.41200061035627,
      "text": "to finishing endpoint, following conventional current.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 194.07200045776838,
      "end": 198.19199938965315,
      "text": "So the total force is I times that endpoint displacement cross B.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 198.71199984741682,
      "end": 201.65200038147444,
      "text": "It is not I times the curved path length times B.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 202.5520000000047,
      "end": 205.25200076294416,
      "text": "The turns in the path make some vector contributions cancel.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 206.5520000000047,
      "end": 211.09200091553205,
      "text": "Consider an upper semicircular wire of radius ten centimeters, carrying three",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 211.09200091553205,
      "end": 213.77200122070784,
      "text": "amperes from its left endpoint to its right endpoint.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 214.69199938965315,
      "end": 219.41200061035627,
      "text": "The entire arc is in a uniform field of zero point two zero tesla into the",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 219.41200061035627,
      "end": 219.69199938965315,
      "text": "page.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 220.85199923706526,
      "end": 223.3920001525926,
      "text": "Endpoint displacement is twenty centimeters to the right.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 224.2320003051805,
      "end": 227.25200076294416,
      "text": "The net force is therefore zero point one two newtons upward.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 228.7320003051805,
      "end": 231.97199816895002,
      "text": "Using the semicircle's arc length would incorrectly add magnitudes without",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 231.97199816895002,
      "end": 233.09200091553205,
      "text": "respecting their directions.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 234.19199938965315,
      "end": 238.21200366211409,
      "text": "If a complete closed loop lies in the same uniform field, starting and ending",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 238.21200366211409,
      "end": 239.09200091553205,
      "text": "points coincide.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 239.91200061035627,
      "end": 241.41200061035627,
      "text": "Its net magnetic force is zero.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 242.33199877930159,
      "end": 246.21200366211409,
      "text": "That does not mean every piece feels zero force, and it does not rule out a",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 246.21200366211409,
      "end": 247.0520000000047,
      "text": "torque that turns the loop.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 247.85200305176252,
      "end": 249.45200152588362,
      "text": "We will treat torque in the next class.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 250.01199908447737,
      "end": 253.67200274658674,
      "text": "If the field varies along the wire, or only part of it lies inside the field,",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 254.2320003051805,
      "end": 257.23200030518046,
      "text": "apply the integral or sum to the actual field covered pieces.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 258.1720027465867,
      "end": 260.33199877930156,
      "text": "Do not extend this shortcut beyond its assumptions.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 260.7600000000072,
      "end": 263.81999994278675,
      "text": "Now let two wires provide each other's magnetic field.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 264.57999993325,
      "end": 268.94410263306776,
      "text": "Assume long, straight, thin parallel wires in vacuum or approximately air,",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 268.94410263306776,
      "end": 269.18000007630116,
      "text": "with",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 269.18000007630116,
      "end": 271.0399997329784,
      "text": "steady currents and negligible end effects.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 271.8200004196239,
      "end": 273.7200000381542,
      "text": "Let their axis separation be d.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 274.6000001525951,
      "end": 279.68000007630116,
      "text": "Wire one produces a field of magnitude mu zero I one divided by two pi d at",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 279.68000007630116,
      "end": 280.2000005340648,
      "text": "wire two.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 280.73999954224354,
      "end": 283.05999923706776,
      "text": "This field is perpendicular to the second wire.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 283.9199998474193,
      "end": 288.33999992371326,
      "text": "Multiply by I two and the selected length L to find the force on that length.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 288.33999992371326,
      "end": 292.940000305183,
      "text": "Force per unit length is mu zero times the product of the current magnitudes",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 292.940000305183,
      "end": 295.3200013732982,
      "text": "divided by two pi d.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 295.3200013732982,
      "end": 295.9199998474193,
      "text": "Where does it point?",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 296.5199983215404,
      "end": 299.1200006103588,
      "text": "Draw both currents upward, with wire one on the left.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 299.6999986267162,
      "end": 301.96000076294666,
      "text": "Its field at the right wire is into the page.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 302.71999908447987,
      "end": 306.379998931892,
      "text": "Upward current cross into page field points left, toward wire one.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 306.8599984741283,
      "end": 308.30000091553455,
      "text": "The left wire is pushed right.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 308.96000076294666,
      "end": 310.30000091553455,
      "text": "Same direction currents attract.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 311.1600015258861,
      "end": 314.98000122071034,
      "text": "Reverse one current and both forces point away from the other wire: opposite",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 314.98000122071034,
      "end": 315.6600015258861,
      "text": "currents repel.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 316.2600000000072,
      "end": 319.940000305183,
      "text": "Use five amperes and eight amperes separated by four centimeters.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 320.8400018310619,
      "end": 325.39999938965565,
      "text": "Taking mu zero approximately four pi times ten to the minus seven gives force",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 325.39999938965565,
      "end": 328.7799966430736,
      "text": "per meter two times ten to the minus four newtons per meter.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 329.49999786377674,
      "end": 333.73076856173725,
      "text": "On a twenty centimeter portion far from the ends, that is four times ten to",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 333.73076856173725,
      "end": 333.89999938965565,
      "text": "the",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 333.89999938965565,
      "end": 334.80000091553455,
      "text": "minus five newtons.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 335.7400033569408,
      "end": 339.4507694890021,
      "text": "Doubling either current doubles the force per meter; doubling separation",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 339.4507694890021,
      "end": 339.7600000000072,
      "text": "halves",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 339.7600000000072,
      "end": 339.98000122071034,
      "text": "it.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 340.49999786377674,
      "end": 344.5200021362377,
      "text": "The wire formula uses axis separation, not the gap between surfaces.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 344.5200021362377,
      "end": 347.88000274658924,
      "text": "Keep the direction check separate from the magnitude calculation.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 348.2160000000096,
      "end": 352.3759998474217,
      "text": "Let's finish with a three wire problem that rewards a careful sketch.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 353.3359998855687,
      "end": 358.29599992371567,
      "text": "Two long fixed parallel wires carry four amperes and six amperes upward,",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 359.2360004577733,
      "end": 360.53599969483383,
      "text": "separated by twenty five centimeters.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 361.29599992371567,
      "end": 364.5760006103612,
      "text": "Put a third parallel wire between them carrying two amperes upward.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 365.31600038147934,
      "end": 367.29599992371567,
      "text": "Both outer wires attract the middle wire.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 368.0560001525975,
      "end": 370.53599969483383,
      "text": "Let x be its distance from the four ampere wire.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 371.17599908448227,
      "end": 374.41600076294907,
      "text": "Its distance from the six ampere wire is d minus x.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 374.7160000000096,
      "end": 378.65600053406723,
      "text": "For zero sideways net force, the two force magnitudes must match.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 379.35599938965805,
      "end": 383.8529240589727,
      "text": "The common factor mu zero I three divided by two pi cancels, leaving I one",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 383.8529240589727,
      "end": 384.09600106812485,
      "text": "over",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 384.09600106812485,
      "end": 386.4560016784764,
      "text": "x equals I two over d minus x.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 387.43600122071274,
      "end": 391.2360004577733,
      "text": "Solve to get x equal to I one d divided by I one plus I two.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 391.7360004577733,
      "end": 395.75600091553696,
      "text": "Here x is ten centimeters, so the other distance is fifteen centimeters.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 396.51599923707016,
      "end": 400.1344626065864,
      "text": "Each attraction is one point six times ten to the minus five newtons per",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 400.1344626065864,
      "end": 400.43600122071274,
      "text": "meter.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 401.1559986267186,
      "end": 404.8960003051854,
      "text": "The balance point is nearer the smaller current, because a smaller source",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 404.8960003051854,
      "end": 407.3960003051854,
      "text": "current needs a shorter distance to give the same field.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 408.4560016784764,
      "end": 411.7960018310643,
      "text": "Now nudge the middle wire slightly right while the outer wires and currents",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 411.7960018310643,
      "end": 412.3960003051854,
      "text": "stay fixed.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 412.9159969482518,
      "end": 415.8159984741307,
      "text": "The rightward attraction grows and the leftward attraction weakens.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 416.5160030517674,
      "end": 420.9159969482518,
      "text": "The wire is pushed farther right: this lateral equilibrium is unstable for our",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 420.9159969482518,
      "end": 422.1160015258885,
      "text": "same direction middle current.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 422.8960003051854,
      "end": 425.1960033569432,
      "text": "Force balance and stability are different questions.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 425.9959987793065,
      "end": 430.0160030517674,
      "text": "Reversing the middle current reverses both pairwise forces without moving the",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 430.0160030517674,
      "end": 430.876003662119,
      "text": "zero force position.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 431.9760021362401,
      "end": 435.735996643076,
      "text": "Finally, if the two outer currents run in opposite directions, their fields",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 435.735996643076,
      "end": 438.0760006103612,
      "text": "between the wires reinforce rather than cancel.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 438.9959987793065,
      "end": 442.83497551708695,
      "text": "There is no intervening zero field balance point for a nonzero third current",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 442.83497551708695,
      "end": 442.93600122071274,
      "text": "in",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 442.93600122071274,
      "end": 443.65600244141586,
      "text": "this ideal model.",
      "chapter": 4
    }
  ]
}
